  "evidence": "- TEXT_D: Leave the EU to restore UK sovereignty; end freedom of movement; Australian-style points system; five-year moratorium on unskilled migration; tighter border enforcement and deportation of foreign offenders; confirm a UK Bill of Rights and withdraw from EU justice measures; spend at least 2% of GDP on defence and retain the nuclear deterrent; repeal the Climate Change Act and end new wind/solar subsidies; sharp reduction in overseas aid.\n- TEXT_G: Protect human rights law and pass a Digital Bill of Rights; decriminalise drug possession and prioritise rehabilitation; remain engaged in and deepen the EU Single Market; maintain 0.7% aid; strong environmental agenda (net-zero by 2050, coal phase-out, green laws, community energy); liberalise asylum/work rules and separate students from migration stats; proportional voting and votes at 16.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_D argues from TAN commitments—national sovereignty over supranational authority, restrictive immigration to protect borders/identity, law-and-order and strong defence—whereas TEXT_G argues from GAL commitments like civil liberties, environmentalism, EU integration, and criminal-justice/drug liberalisation. Because the electorate profile leans TAN (0.60 TAN vs 0.40 GAL), TEXT_D aligns more closely than the strongly GAL‑oriented TEXT_G.",
